<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint-Paulien</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ruessium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint-Paulien is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haute-loire">Haute-Loire</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-puy-en-velay">Le Puy-en-Velay</a>. Under Roman rule, as Ruessium, it was the chief town of the Gallic Vellavi and the capital of their civitas. In late antiquity the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> moved to Anicium, Le Puy-en-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velay">Velay</a>, and Ruessium declined into a village, though Roman remains and inscriptions survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-paulien.jpg" alt="Saint-Paulien" width="320" height="280" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MOSSOT · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint-Paulien_-_Coll%C3%A9giale_Saint-Georges_-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
